1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,260
You want explainer videos about your code.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:05,660
The first question is where they live.

3
00:00:06,410 --> 00:00:11,170
The tempting answer is to add Lyceum to your app's dependencies, like any other tool.

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:16,030
But Lyceum brings a whole toolchain with it: a browser driver, a bundler,

5
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:18,290
a type checker, and an interface library.

6
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:21,930
Where your app uses the same tools, the versions can collide.

7
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:27,050
And if your app is written in Go or Swift, there's no dependencies file to add

8
00:00:27,050 --> 00:00:27,610
it to at all.

9
00:00:29,533 --> 00:00:33,333
Instead, give the videos a small project of their own.

10
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:40,533
Lyceum's setup command creates a folder, usually called explainers, with its own dependencies,

11
00:00:40,893 --> 00:00:43,913
a guide for the agent, and a folder for the videos.

12
00:00:44,813 --> 00:00:46,953
Your app never sees any of it.

13
00:00:47,703 --> 00:00:53,463
In a JavaScript monorepo, keep that folder out of the workspace, and out of the root

14
00:00:53,463 --> 00:00:56,103
type check, linting and continuous integration.

15
00:00:56,503 --> 00:00:58,663
It installs and checks itself.

16
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:04,287
If the videos explain one codebase to the people who work on it,

17
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:09,167
put that folder in the same repository, and treat it like documentation.

18
00:01:09,917 --> 00:01:15,037
The agent's ground truth is right next door, at the same commit the video describes,

19
00:01:15,257 --> 00:01:16,297
in any checkout.

20
00:01:17,317 --> 00:01:21,677
Spotting a stale video is just a diff: each script lists the code it explains,

21
00:01:22,057 --> 00:01:25,357
and the history shows what has changed since the video was checked.

22
00:01:25,797 --> 00:01:26,737
And it's light.

23
00:01:27,057 --> 00:01:30,897
What gets committed is the scripts, the scenes and the narration timings:

24
00:01:31,097 --> 00:01:34,217
a little over a hundred kilobytes for a three-minute video.

25
00:01:34,617 --> 00:01:37,077
The cost is a little noise in your pull requests.

26
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,360
Give the videos a repository of their own when they span several repositories,

27
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,480
or a whole product.

28
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:48,340
The same goes when they're for someone other than the code's contributors,

29
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:50,540
or they're your own learning material.

30
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:54,610
Then the project guide points at the other repositories by path.

31
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:59,250
So every author needs them checked out side by side, in the same layout.

32
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:01,490
Write that down in the guide.

33
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:05,460
Each author sets up their own machine.

34
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:08,060
First, give the coding agent the skill.

35
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:13,340
By default, it's linked for your whole user account, pointing at one project's copy of Lyceum.

36
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:17,680
With two projects on different versions, the agent only sees the first.

37
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,440
So link it for one repository instead, and keep the link out of version control,

38
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:24,060
since it points at your own install.

39
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:26,950
Second, a voice key.

40
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:31,630
Keep it in a key file inside the project, and make sure version control ignores it.

41
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:36,333
Now the part to know before you share with a team.

42
00:02:36,513 --> 00:02:40,093
The scripts, the scenes and the word timings travel with the repository.

43
00:02:40,393 --> 00:02:42,153
The narration audio doesn't.

44
00:02:42,353 --> 00:02:44,313
It stays on the machine that voiced it.

45
00:02:45,063 --> 00:02:50,303
A teammate who clones the project can still build scenes and check them on contact sheets,

46
00:02:50,483 --> 00:02:52,623
because those only need the timings.

47
00:02:52,983 --> 00:02:55,563
But rendering the video needs the audio.

48
00:02:56,143 --> 00:02:59,163
Voicing it again costs money and gives a different take.

49
00:02:59,543 --> 00:03:03,363
The timing shifts, and animations drift away from what was reviewed.

50
00:03:04,113 --> 00:03:08,113
So for now, let one person own the narration and the final render.

51
00:03:08,733 --> 00:03:11,053
Everyone else sends changes as pull requests.

52
00:03:13,067 --> 00:03:18,207
Last, decide where viewers find the videos, because the finished renders aren't committed either.

53
00:03:18,547 --> 00:03:24,047
Release assets, a documentation site, or attached to the pull request that changes what the video

54
00:03:24,047 --> 00:03:24,487
explains.

55
00:03:24,807 --> 00:03:26,427
Write the answer in the project guide.

56
00:03:27,177 --> 00:03:32,197
So: the videos get their own small project, beside the code they explain.

57
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:34,617
The skill is linked per repository.

58
00:03:35,157 --> 00:03:37,057
And one person owns the voice.
